  "b81a0154-723b-4eb5-b9a6-2b4ac5cf8b3c"],
 "\nThe file is a 2-dimensional array where each row is the set of all \
possible colorings for a given cap.  This results in a list of 3744 distinct \
caps with a total of 7210 colored caps for them.  In the table below, the \
data is formatted in rows so that the first column contains the cap number (a \
value in the range from 1 to 3744), the second column contains an index to \
identify the colored cap for that cap, the third provides the colored cap, \
and the fourth gives a 4-face coloring of some state of the Birkhoff diamond \
that is compatible with the colored cap. Here compatible means that the \
4-face coloring on the capped state of the Birkhoff diamond in column 4, when \
restricted to just the cap, is the same coloring as the colored cap in column \
3 except that at most two arcs (that do not have interactions) are colored \
the fourth color (yellow).  Note that the construction of the coloring on the \
capped state may involve renaming the interactions (the V[a,b] terms) in \
order to eliminate redundant interactions.  Therefore some of the \
interactions from the cap may not directly appear in the coloring of the \
capped states.  This list proves Proposition 5.1 in the article, which as \
explained in Section 4 of the article, proves that the Birkhoff diamond is \
state-reducible according to Definition 1.3. This, together with Theorem 1, \
implies Theorem 2, that is, the Birkhoff diamond cannot show up as a \
configuration in a minimal counter example to the four color theorem.\n\nTo \
explain the notation in the table, ",
 StyleBox["FPResultsIndexed",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", we take the third coloring of the 47th cap as indicated by the following \
row:\n\n",
 Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{
    {"47", "3", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"arc", "[", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", 
        RowBox[{"A", "[", 
