   DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
   ImageSize->{648.06640625, Automatic},
   ImageSizeRaw->{441., 390.},
   PlotRange->{{0, 441.}, {0, 390.}}]], "Input",ExpressionUUID->
  "43e63d10-c92e-45e9-907c-be471b09136b"],
 "\nThe interaction sites (the V[a,b] terms) include both the interactions in \
the cap, and those that occur along the edges of the Birkhoff diamond. We \
need all interactions (both in the cap and in the configuration) to check \
that the coloring is proper.\n\nTo check to see whether a coloring is proper \
or not, we provide a replacement rule that serves as a check that the \
coloring provided is valid.  The rule is given below as ",
 StyleBox["checkColor",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".  It works by simply checking for a pair of circles that are the same \
color, together with an interaction between the two circles.  If it finds \
such an occurrence, it zeros out the coloring.  One may use this rule on a \
case-by-case basis, say for the example above, by running the following \
command:\n\nFPResults[[47,3]]//.checkColor\n\nIf the coloring is proper, then \
the function returns the entire coloring, unaltered (it \
didn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t zero out the coloring).  This example is included in \
the code below.\n\nSuppose, for the sake of showing a bad coloring, that one \
modifies the coloring above by switching the yellow arc to red as shown. \
Notice that this causes two red circles that pass through the Birkhoff \
diamond to interact with each other along two edges.\n  \n  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  GraphicsBox[
   TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["
