     "], {{0, 449.}, {578., 0}}, {0, 255},
     ColorFunction->RGBColor,
     ImageResolution->{144., 144.}],
    BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True],
    Selectable->False],
   DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
   ImageSizeRaw->{578., 449.},
   PlotRange->{{0, 578.}, {0, 449.}}]], "Input",ExpressionUUID->
  "ce730b02-d14b-4f98-bf6e-aa46e7074e35"],
 "\nThis coloring is given by replacing the 4 for the yellow arc to 1 to make \
it a red arc:\n\nBadColoring =  arc[1,A[4,9,16,21,28,38,40]] \
arc[2,A[2,11,15,34]] arc[2,A[6,7,26,48]] arc[2,A[3,10,20,22,31,41,43]] \
arc[3,A[5,8,13,17,19,24,27,29,33,36,39,45,47]] \
arc[1,A[1,12,14,18,23,25,30,32,35,37,42,44,46]] V[1,2] V[1,3] V[1,4] V[1,5] \
V[1,6] V[2,5] V[3,4] V[3,5] V[4,5] V[4,6] V[5,6]\n\nIf we run the command,\n\n\
BadColoring//.checkColor\n\nwe see that Mathematica returns 0, indicated that \
the coloring is not valid.  This example is included in the code below.\n\nWe \
can use the checkColor rule on the entire list of results using a single \
command:\n\nFPResults//.checkColor\n\nChecking this list for the existence of \
any zeros amounts to checking whether or not any of the colorings are \
invalid.  This can be difficult since the list in this form is hard to read.  \
However, we can have Mathematica check the list for us with the command: \n\n\
Position[FPResults//.checkColor,0]\n\nThe function returns an empty list, \
indicating that no invalid colorings were found, that is to say, all of the \
included colorings are valid.\n\nNext, we generate a table that checks that a \
3-face coloring of a cap of the reducer is contained within a single circle \
of the corresponding capped state of the Birkhoff diamond, with either the \
same color, or yellow.  This ensures that the coloring of the capped state of \
the Birkhoff diamond is compatible with the corresponding colored cap of the \
reducer.  Every entry of the table should be True, to indicate that the cap \
matches the capped state.  This is the check of state-extendible. \n\n\
Finally, we build the table, ",
 StyleBox["FPResultsIndexed, ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "from ",
 StyleBox["ColoredCaps",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["FPResults ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "that is partially shown in Table 1 of article",
 StyleBox[". ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "It shows explicitly the correspondence between the colored cap from a \
3-face coloring for a capped state on the reducer and the corresponding \
4-face coloring for a capped state on the Birkhoff diamond. Note  that there \
are often ",
 StyleBox["many",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " capped states on the Birkhoff diamond that have a 4-face coloring, but to \
prove Proposition 5.1, we need to only exhibit one. (There is a separate \
program, not provided here,  that inputs ",
 StyleBox["ColoredCaps",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " and outputs ",
 StyleBox["FPResults",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". It is not provided here because it is proprietorially needed  for future \
research.)  The Do-Loop below builds the table, merging each entry in ",
 StyleBox["ColoredCaps",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " with the corresponding entry in ",
 StyleBox["FPResults",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". Since we have already checked that each 4-face coloring of each capped \
state on the Birkhoff diamond is proper, and that each entry in column for a \
3-face coloring of a cap on the reducer corresponds to the 4-face coloring of \
capped state on the BIrkhoff diamond, one can visually inspect the 7210 \
entries quickly to verify Proposition 5.1."
}], "Text",
